{"timestamp":"2026-07-22T10:07:21.170Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<permissions instructions>\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `danger-full-access`: No filesystem sandboxing - all commands are permitted. Network access is enabled.\nApproval policy is currently never. Do not provide the `sandbox_permissions` for any reason, commands will be rejected.\n</permissions instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<skills_instructions>\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of instructions provided through a `SKILL.md` source. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and source locator. `file` locators are on the host filesystem, `environment resource` locators are owned by an execution environment, `orchestrator resource` locators are opaque non-filesystem resources, and `custom resource` locators use their provider's access mechanism.\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: /home/agent/.codex/skills/.system/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use when the user asks how to build with OpenAI products or APIs, asks about Codex itself or choosing Codex surfaces, needs up-to-date official documentation with citations, help choosing the latest model for a use case, or model upgrade and prompt-upgrade guidance; use OpenAI docs MCP tools for non-Codex docs questions, use the Codex manual helper first for broad Codex self-knowledge, and restrict fallback browsing to official OpenAI domains. (file: /home/agent/.codex/skills/.system/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: /home/agent/.codex/skills/.system/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Guide for creating effective skills. This skill should be used when users want to create a new skill (or update an existing skill) that extends Codex's capabilities with specialized knowledge, workflows, or tool integrations. (file: /home/agent/.codex/skills/.system/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: /home/agent/.codex/skills/.system/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n</skills_instructions>"},{"type":"input_text","text":"<apps_instructions>\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n</apps_instructions>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f894b-462f-7d20-94c1-769380a750cb"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-22T10:07:21.170Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"developer","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"You are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: <recipient>\nSender: <author>\nPayload:\n<payload text>\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f894b-462f-7d20-94c1-769380a750cb"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-22T10:17:31.944Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"call_Gh3gy9akkQMwYtxKZ0cohBjX","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"prove.sh\nsemantic.k\nsolution.mpy\nsolution.py\nspec.k\nverification.k\n     1\tdef below_threshold(l: list, t: int):\n     2\t    for x in l:\n     3\t        if x >= t:\n     4\t            return False\n     5\t    return True\n     1\tmodule MPY-SYNTAX\n     2\t  imports INT-SYNTAX\n     3\t  imports BOOL-SYNTAX\n     4\t  imports STRING-SYNTAX\n     5\t\n     6\t  // The source subset emitted by py2mpy.py for solution.py.\n     7\t  syntax Pgm    ::= \"Module\" \"(\" Stmts \")\"\n     8\t  syntax Stmts  ::= List{Stmt, \"\"}\n     9\t  syntax Stmt   ::= \"FuncDef\" \"(\" String \",\" Params \",\" Stmts \")\"\n    10\t                  | \"For\" \"(\" Expr \",\" Expr \",\" Stmts \")\"\n    11\t                  | \"If\" \"(\" Expr \",\" Stmts \",\" Stmts \")\"\n    12\t                  | \"Return\" \"(\" Expr \")\"\n    13\t  syntax ParamItems ::= List{String, \",\"}\n    14\t  syntax Params ::= \"Params\" \"(\" ParamItems \")\"\n    15\t  syntax Expr   ::= \"Name\" \"(\" String \")\"\n    16\t                  | \"Bool\" \"(\" Bool \")\"\n    17\t                  | \"Compare\" \"(\" Expr \",\" CmpOp \")\"\n    18\t  syntax CmpOp  ::= \"CmpOp\" \"(\" String \",\" Expr \")\"\n    19\t\n    20\t  // A recursive input representation gives the prover an inductive shape.\n    21\t  syntax IntSeq ::= \"nil\"\n    22\t                  | \"cons\" \"(\" Int \",\" IntSeq \")\"\n    23\tendmodule\n    24\t\n    25\tmodule MPY\n    26\t  imports MPY-SYNTAX\n    27\t  imports INT\n    28\t  imports BOOL\n    29\t\n    30\t  syntax Value ::= VInt(Int) | VBool(Bool) | VList(IntSeq)\n    31\t  syntax Slot ::= \"unbound\" | \"slot\" \"(\" Value \")\"\n    32\t  syntax Result ::= \"noResult\" | \"result\" \"(\" Bool \")\"\n    33\t\n    34\t  syntax KItem ::= \"boot\"\n    35\t                 | \"exec\" \"(\" Stmts \")\"\n    36\t                 | \"eval\" \"(\" Expr \")\"\n    37\t                 | \"ifK\" \"(\" Stmts \",\" Stmts \")\"\n    38\t                 | \"forK\" \"(\" Expr \",\" Stmts \")\"\n    39\t                 | \"loop\" \"(\" Expr \",\" IntSeq \",\" Stmts \")\"\n    40\t                 | \"cmpRight\" \"(\" Expr \")\"\n    41\t                 | \"cmpValues\" \"(\" Value \",\" String \")\"\n    42\t                 | \"returnK\"\n    43\t\n    44\t  configuration\n    45\t    <bt>\n    46\t      <k> boot </k>\n    47\t      <program> $PGM:Pgm </program>\n    48\t      <input> $INPUT:IntSeq </input>\n    49\t      <threshold> $THRESHOLD:Int </threshold>\n    50\t      <l> unbound </l>\n    51\t      <t> unbound </t>\n    52\t      <x> unbound </x>\n    53\t      <result> noResult </result>\n    54\t    </bt>\n    55\t\n    56\t  // Loading the module and invoking its required two-argument entry point.\n    57\t  rule <k> boot => exec(BODY) ... </k>\n    58\t       <program>\n    59\t         Module(FuncDef(\"below_threshold\", Params(\"l\", \"t\"), BODY))\n    60\t       </program>\n    61\t       <input> XS </input>\n    62\t       <threshold> T </threshold>\n    63\t       <l> unbound => slot(VList(XS)) </l>\n    64\t       <t> unbound => slot(VInt(T)) </t>\n    65\t\n    66\t  // Sequential statement execution.\n    67\t  rule <k> exec(.Stmts) => .K ... </k>\n    68\t  rule <k> exec(S:Stmt SS:Stmts) => S ~> exec(SS) ... </k>\n    69\t\n    70\t  // Expression evaluation and environment lookup.\n    71\t  rule <k> eval(Bool(B)) => VBool(B) ... </k>\n    72\t  rule <k> eval(Name(\"l\")) => V ... </k>\n    73\t       <l> slot(V) </l>\n    74\t  rule <k> eval(Name(\"t\")) => V ... </k>\n    75\t       <t> slot(V) </t>\n    76\t  rule <k> eval(Name(\"x\")) => V ... </k>\n    77\t       <x> slot(V) </x>\n    78\t\n    79\t  // The comparison shape used by the implementation, evaluated left-to-right.\n    80\t  rule <k> eval(Compare(E1, CmpOp(\">=\", E2)))\n    81\t        => eval(E1) ~> cmpRight(E2) ... </k>\n    82\t  rule <k> V1:Value ~> cmpRight(E2)\n    83\t        => eval(E2) ~> cmpValues(V1, \">=\") ... </k>\n    84\t  rule <k> VInt(I2) ~> cmpValues(VInt(I1), \">=\")\n    85\t        => VBool(I1 >=Int I2) ... </k>\n    86\t\n    87\t  // Conditional statements execute exactly one branch.\n    88\t  rule <k> If(COND, THEN, ELSE)\n    89\t        => eval(COND) ~> ifK(THEN, ELSE) ... </k>\n    90\t  rule <k> VBool(true) ~> ifK(THEN, _ELSE)\n    91\t        => exec(THEN) ... </k>\n    92\t  rule <k> VBool(false) ~> ifK(_THEN, ELSE)\n    93\t        => exec(ELSE) ... </k>\n    94\t\n    95\t  // For loops consume the iterable from left to right and bind the target.\n    96\t  rule <k> For(TARGET, ITER, BODY)\n    97\t        => eval(ITER) ~> forK(TARGET, BODY) ... </k>\n    98\t  rule <k> VList(XS) ~> forK(TARGET, BODY)\n    99\t        => loop(TARGET, XS, BODY) ... </k>\n   100\t  rule <k> loop(_TARGET, nil, _BODY) => .K ... </k>\n   101\t  rule <k> loop(Name(\"x\"), cons(I, XS), BODY)\n   102\t        => exec(BODY) ~> loop(Name(\"x\"), XS, BODY) ... </k>\n   103\t       <x> _ => slot(VInt(I)) </x>\n   104\t\n   105\t  // Return discards the rest of the current function computation.\n   106\t  rule <k> Return(E) ~> _REST => eval(E) ~> returnK </k>\n   107\t  rule <k> VBool(B) ~> returnK => .K </k>\n   108\t       <result> noResult => result(B) </result>\n   109\tendmodule\n     1\trequires \"semantic.k\"\n     2\t\n     3\tmodule VERIFICATION\n     4\t  imports MPY\n     5\t\n     6\t  // Mathematical contract: every element is strictly below the threshold.\n     7\t  syntax Bool ::= allBelow(IntSeq, Int) [function, total]\n     8\t  rule allBelow(nil, _T) => true\n     9\t  rule allBelow(cons(I, XS), T)\n    10\t    => (I <Int T) andBool allBelow(XS, T)\n    11\t\n    12\t  // One canonical spelling of the exact translated solution, shared by claims.\n    13\t  syntax Pgm ::= \"solutionProgram\" [macro]\n    14\t  rule solutionProgram\n    15\t    => Module(\n    16\t         FuncDef(\"below_threshold\", Params(\"l\", \"t\"),\n    17\t           For(Name(\"x\"), Name(\"l\"),\n    18\t             If(Compare(Name(\"x\"), CmpOp(\">=\", Name(\"t\"))),\n    19\t               Return(Bool(false)),\n    20\t               .Stmts))\n    21\t           Return(Bool(true))))\n    22\tendmodule\n     1\trequires \"verification.k\"\n     2\t\n     3\tmodule SPEC\n     4\t  imports VERIFICATION\n     5\t\n     6\t  // Inductive loop invariant.  If the suffix XS remains to be scanned, the\n     7\t  // eventual answer is exactly the mathematical predicate on that suffix.\n     8\t  claim [loop-invariant]:\n     9\t    <bt>\n    10\t      <k>\n    11\t        loop(Name(\"x\"), XS:IntSeq,\n    12\t          If(Compare(Name(\"x\"), CmpOp(\">=\", Name(\"t\"))),\n    13\t            Return(Bool(false)),\n    14\t            .Stmts))\n    15\t        ~> exec(Return(Bool(true)) .Stmts)\n    16\t        => .K\n    17\t      </k>\n    18\t      <program> solutionProgram </program>\n    19\t      <input> INPUT:IntSeq </input>\n    20\t      <threshold> T:Int </threshold>\n    21\t      <l> slot(VList(INPUT)) </l>\n    22\t      <t> slot(VInt(T)) </t>\n    23\t      <x> _INITIAL_X:Slot => ?_FINAL_LOOP_X:Slot </x>\n    24\t      <result> noResult => result(allBelow(XS, T)) </result>\n    25\t    </bt>\n    26\t\n    27\t  claim [below-threshold-correct]:\n    28\t    <bt>\n    29\t      <k> boot => .K </k>\n    30\t      <program> solutionProgram </program>\n    31\t      <input> XS:IntSeq </input>\n    32\t      <threshold> T:Int </threshold>\n    33\t      <l> unbound => slot(VList(XS)) </l>\n    34\t      <t> unbound => slot(VInt(T)) </t>\n    35\t      <x> unbound => ?_FINAL_X:Slot </x>\n    36\t      <result> noResult => result(allBelow(XS, T)) </result>\n    37\t    </bt>\n    38\tendmodule\n     1\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n     2\tset -euo pipefail\n     3\t\n     4\t# Regenerate the constructor term from the submitted Python implementation.\n     5\tpython3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy\n     6\t\n     7\t# Check the prompt examples and two boundary cases in CPython.\n     8\tpython3 - <<'PY'\n     9\tfrom solution import below_threshold\n    10\t\n    11\tassert below_threshold([1, 2, 4, 10], 100) is True\n    12\tassert below_threshold([1, 20, 4, 10], 5) is False\n    13\tassert below_threshold([], -100) is True\n    14\tassert below_threshold([5], 5) is False\n    15\tprint(\"CPython tests: passed\")\n    16\tPY\n    17\t\n    18\t# Compile the handwritten semantics together with the verification helpers.\n    19\tkompile verification.k \\\n    20\t  --main-module VERIFICATION \\\n    21\t  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\n    22\t  --backend haskell\n    23\t\n    24\t# Exercise the translated program through the semantics.\n    25\tkrun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled \\\n    26\t  -cINPUT='cons(1, cons(2, cons(4, cons(10, nil))))' \\\n    27\t  -cTHRESHOLD=100\n    28\tkrun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled \\\n    29\t  -cINPUT='cons(1, cons(20, cons(4, cons(10, nil))))' \\\n    30\t  -cTHRESHOLD=5\n    31\tkrun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled \\\n    32\t  -cINPUT='nil' \\\n    33\t  -cTHRESHOLD=-100\n    34\tkrun solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled \\\n    35\t  -cINPUT='cons(5, nil)' \\\n    36\t  -cTHRESHOLD=5\n    37\t\n    38\t# Prove every claim in spec.k; success prints #Top and exits zero.\n    39\tkprove spec.k \\\n    40\t  --definition verification-kompiled \\\n    41\t  --spec-module SPEC \\\n    42\t  --output pretty\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f894b-462f-7d20-94c1-769380a750cb"}}}
